The queue here stores array indexes, not separate tree nodes. I start with index `0`, check its children, and then add those child indexes to the queue. This follows the tree level by level.

For `[1, 2, 5, 4, 7]`, index `0` is checked first, then indexes `1` and `2`. All parent values are smaller, so the array passes.

```javascript
class Solution {
    isHeap(nums) {
        if (nums.length === 0) {
            return true;
        }

        const queue = [0];
        let head = 0;

        while (head < queue.length) {
            const parent = queue[head++];
            const left = 2 * parent + 1;
            const right = 2 * parent + 2;

            if (left < nums.length) {
                if (nums[parent] > nums[left]) {
                    return false;
                }
                queue.push(left);
            }

            if (right < nums.length) {
                if (nums[parent] > nums[right]) {
                    return false;
                }
                queue.push(right);
            }
        }

        return true;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the index queue. The original array is not modified.

The array describes a complete binary tree, so the heap rule can be checked recursively. Starting at an index, I compare it with its left and right children, then continue with both subtrees.

An empty array and a one-element array are valid because they have no parent-child pair to break the rule.

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean isHeap(int[] nums) {
        return check(nums, 0);
    }

    private boolean check(int[] nums, int index) {
        if (index >= nums.length) {
            return true;
        }

        int left = 2 * index + 1;
        int right = 2 * index + 2;

        if (left < nums.length && nums[index] > nums[left]) {
            return false;
        }
        if (right < nums.length && nums[index] > nums[right]) {
            return false;
        }

        return check(nums, left) && check(nums, right);
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(h)` for the recursive call stack, where `h` is the tree height. The array itself is unchanged.

Instead of looping over parents, this version visits each child and finds its parent directly. Equal values are allowed, so only a child smaller than its parent causes failure.

A quick dry run on `[2, 4, 3, 9, 6]` gives parent values `2, 2, 4, 4`; every child is at least as large as its parent.

```python
class Solution:
    def isHeap(self, nums):
        for child in range(1, len(nums)):
            parent = (child - 1) // 2
            if nums[child] < nums[parent]:
                return False
        return True
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)`. No values are added, removed, or changed in `nums`.

I checked every node against its parent. In a zero-based array, the parent of index `i` is `(i - 1) / 2`. If any child is smaller than its parent, the array is not a min heap.

For example, for `[1, 3, 2, 7, 8]`, the checks are `1 <= 3`, `1 <= 2`, `3 <= 7`, and `3 <= 8`, so the result is true.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    bool isHeap(vector<int>& nums) {
        for (int i = 1; i < static_cast<int>(nums.size()); ++i) {
            int parent = (i - 1) / 2;
            if (nums[parent] > nums[i]) {
                return false;
            }
        }
        return true;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)` because each child is checked once.
Space complexity: `O(1)`. The input array is not changed.

Only internal nodes have children, so the loop stops at the last index that can have a child. Each node is compared with its left and right child when those children exist.

This also handles an array with just a root. The loop does not run, and that array is correctly accepted.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    bool isHeap(vector<int>& nums) {
        int n = static_cast<int>(nums.size());

        for (int parent = 0; parent <= (n - 2) / 2; ++parent) {
            int left = 2 * parent + 1;
            int right = 2 * parent + 2;

            if (nums[parent] > nums[left]) {
                return false;
            }
            if (right < n && nums[parent] > nums[right]) {
                return false;
            }
        }

        return true;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`, with each possible child relation checked once.
Space complexity: `O(1)`. The input is read only.

## Intuition
Iterate over each node once to check if there exists any node smaller that violates the min-heap property

## Approach
Brute Force Approach O(N)
Here, we are trying to iterate over individual nodes onces, for checking if there exists any node in left subtree and right substree which have a value greater than the root node. 

Optimised Approach O(N/2)
Instead, of iterating over individual nodes, we can iterate over non-leaf node which carry the min-heap property as the leaf nodes are the endpoints, they automatically follow the min-heap property with no subtree below them.

## Solution
```java[]
class Solution {
    public boolean isHeap(int[] nums) {
        int n = nums.length;

       // Iterate over the non-leaf nodes
       for(int i= (int)Math.floor(n / 2.0) - 1; i>=0; i--) {
            int l = 2 * i + 1;
            int r = 2 * i + 2;

            if(l < n && nums[l] < nums[i]) return false;
            if(r < n && nums[r] < nums[i]) return false;    
       }

       return true;
    }

    public boolean isHeap(int[] nums, int ind) {
        int n = nums.length;
        if(ind >= n) return true;

        int s = ind;
        int l = 2 * ind + 1;
        int r = 2 * ind + 2;

        if(l >= 0 && l < n && nums[l] < nums[s]) s = l;
        if(r >= 0 && r < n && nums[r] < nums[s]) s = r;

        if(s != ind) return false;

        boolean isLftValidHeap = isHeap(nums, l);
        boolean isRhtValidHeap = isHeap(nums, r);

        return isLftValidHeap && isRhtValidHeap;
    }
}
```

## Complexity
**Time Complexity:** 
O(N)

**Space Complexity:**
O(log N)
